<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ksar es-Seghir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alcácer Ceguer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ksar es-Seghir is a small coastal town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>. A medieval port for crossings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, it was captured by Portugal under Afonso V in 1458 and held until 1550. The excavated remains of its Islamic town and Portuguese fortress survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ksar-es-seghir.jpg" alt="Ksar es-Seghir" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boubloub · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morocco_KsarelSeghir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
